&lt;div id="load-packages" class="section level1" number="1">
&lt;h1>&lt;span class="header-section-number">1&lt;/span> Load packages&lt;/h1>
&lt;pre class="r">&lt;code>library(tidyverse) # data wrangling&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>
&lt;/div>
&lt;div id="introduction" class="section level1" number="2">
&lt;h1>&lt;span class="header-section-number">2&lt;/span> Introduction&lt;/h1>
&lt;p>In this post, I want to show you how to work with list columns in R. List columns are a powerful feature of the &lt;code>tidyverse&lt;/code> that allow you to store multiple objects in a single column of a data frame. This can be useful when you have a list of objects that you want to keep together, such as a list of data frames or a list of models.&lt;/p></description></item></channel></rss>